						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-10-19T07:19:14.994981+11:00"
					}
				},
				"editEmail" : {
					"__type" : "email",
					"emailString" : "<unknown>"
				},
				"editTime" : {
					"__type" : "time",
					"time" : {
						"__type" : "dateAndTime",
						"dateAndTimeString" : "2026-10-19T07:19:14.994981+11:00"
					}
				},
				"uid" : {
					"__type" : "uid",
					"uidString" : "+ICdIGP2QSacdkQY3/9Q5Q=="
				},
				"paragraphStyle" : {
					"__type" : "textStyle"
				},
				"string" : "## Barry-local Codex connection lifecycle — image implementation\nOwner: CatalystDevKit, BV-Catalyst-Fairy-Core (test doubles and examples in its Examples tag). {{gtClass:BVCFairyCodexClient}} specialises only the fresh-request operation; inherited credentials, request headers/options, OAuth retry, SSE parser and history remain in use. {{gtMethod:BVCFairyChat>>#buildDefaultProvider}} and {{gtMethod:BVCFairyChat>>#provider:}} install through model client:. Other providers and ordinary GT chats are unchanged. Existing Barry client consumers were migrated together through their model client: seam, retaining history and all client slots by identity; root and child providers/models were not replaced. Children inherit the installed shared client through the existing agent builder.\n\nThe request invocation owns its fresh Zinc client and closes it in ensure: after success, error or process unwind. A fully read response keeps the original response/headers, replaces only its consumed entity with text body evidence, and retains the usual parsed result/history. GT request/response views and history export consumers were inspected: they need response contents/serialization rather than a live transport. SSE is still SSE, not JSON; the generic JSON-tree view cannot parse SSE. Old consumed bodies cannot be reconstructed. Partial body-read/network errors retain their original error evidence, not an invented complete body; this change does not add failure records to history.\n\nZinc beOneShot explicitly rejects streaming. Zinc close closes its connection; secure stream close destroys its SSL session and underlying Socket, unregistering its three external semaphores. No GT or Zinc source was edited. Remove this narrowly scoped override and installation methods when upstream Codex provides equivalent guaranteed per-request close/unwind and inspectable materialized body evidence; rerun these examples before removal.\n\nCancellation limit: existing provider stop is cooperative (running state becomes stopping); it does not interrupt an HTTP read. This change closes when that invocation returns/errors/unwinds, not immediately on a UI stop. Process termination unwind is executable-tested. Prompt native read interruption on cooperative cancel remains a gap, not a claimed capability."
			},
			{
				"__type" : "textSnippet",
				"children" : {
					"__type" : "snippets",
					"items" : [ ]
				},
